      "content": "Flexbox's Limitation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In the previous chapter, you learned that flexbox is a one-dimensional layout system that allows arranging items in either rows or columns."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "But suppose you want to create a layout that looks like this:"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Flexbox's Limitation",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "To create the above layout using flexbox, you would need to use three separate wrappers (each indicated by dark borders) like this:"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Flexbox's Limitation",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Thankfully, you can use grids to create the same layout with a single container."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
